INT. FERRY TERMINAL WAITING ROOM - IMPROVISED CLINIC - NIGHT

A blizzard rattles the windows. Rows of molded plastic chairs have been pushed aside to make room for six camp cots. A vending machine glows in the corner. Blankets, IV poles borrowed from a stalled ambulance, a whiteboard on which someone has written TRIAGE in red marker.

DR. SOLVEIG ABIODUN, forties, in a parka over green scrubs, kneels beside a cot where an OLD MAN lies grey-faced under three blankets. Behind her, NURSE PRAKASH IYENGAR, thirties, tapes a gauze pad to a young woman's arm. At the doorway, PARAMEDIC LUCIA FERREIRA-HOLT, twenties, stamps snow from her boots and drops an equipment bag.

SOLVEIG
(to the room)
I need quiet. All of you. Quiet.

The room hushes. Wind howls. A vending machine hums.

LUCIA
Road's closed. Plow can't get through before dawn.

SOLVEIG
How long is that?

LUCIA
Nine hours. Maybe ten.

SOLVEIG
(to herself)
Ten.

PRAKASH
Doctor, I'm out of saline.

SOLVEIG
How many bags?

PRAKASH
Two.

SOLVEIG
Use one on him. Save the last.

PRAKASH
For?

SOLVEIG
For whoever's worse.

She stands. Her knees crack. She crosses the room to the whiteboard and writes a number beside the first name.

SOLVEIG (CONT'D)
(to Lucia)
Pressure?

LUCIA
Ninety over fifty.

SOLVEIG
Pulse?

LUCIA
One-thirty.

SOLVEIG
That's not a stable man.

LUCIA
He was fine an hour ago.

SOLVEIG
They always are.

PRAKASH
(low, to Solveig)
Doctor, there's something you should see.

She follows him to the corner cot. The young woman Prakash was tending lifts her sleeve. Under the gauze, the skin is bruised black from elbow to wrist.

SOLVEIG
How long has she been like this?

PRAKASH
She says since the bus.

SOLVEIG
What bus?

LUCIA
(from across the room)
The one that skidded on the bridge. She walked here.

SOLVEIG
She walked here on that arm?

LUCIA
Four miles.

SOLVEIG
(shaking her head)
Everyone in this room is lying about how bad it is.

PRAKASH
Including you.

SOLVEIG
Including me.

The lights flicker. Everyone looks up. The vending machine goes dark. A groan from the old man's cot. Lucia runs to him. Solveig stays where she is, watching the ceiling lights hum back to life.

SOLVEIG (CONT'D)
(to the whole room)
Nobody panic. We have generators. We have blankets. We have each other, and I know that sounds like a poster.

A weak laugh from one of the cots.

SOLVEIG (CONT'D)
(quiet, to Prakash)
Get me the last bag.

PRAKASH
For her?

SOLVEIG
For him.

LUCIA
(calling)
Doctor! His pressure's dropping!

Solveig is already moving.
